      "pageTitle": "Fact Check: NO Uniform '15% Band' Formula Mapping Trump's 2024 Result Exactly Onto 2026 Senate Race -- Results Show Variations",
      "pageDescription": "Does a viral graph mapping President Trump&#039;s result in the 2024 election onto the results of the 2026 Senate election...",
      "evidenceText": "Fact Check: NO Uniform '15% Band' Formula Mapping Trump's 2024 Result Exactly Onto 2026 Senate Race -- Results Show Variations | Lead Stories STORY UPDATED: check for updates below . More Variation Does a viral graph mapping President Trump's result in the 2024 election onto the results of the 2026 Senate election show a uniform, 15% decrease for all states that had a Senate election? No, that's not true: A clearer version of the graph produced by Lead Stories shows only a few states where the number from that calculation matches the actual result within one or two percent, all won by Trump in 2024 and by the Republican candidate in 2026. All the actual results in the other states show variation in both directions, showing much higher or lower numbers than the ones the formula returns. The graph appeared in a post on X (archived here ) published by \"Truth is Freedom\" on November 6, 2026, which read: THE 15% BAND Look at this chart. 59 races. Senate and governor. States ordered from reddest to bluest in 2024. The solid lines are 2024. The dashed lines are 2026. The shaded bands are what moved. One formula fits all of it: Republican 2026 = 0.85 × Trump 2024 Democrat 2026 = Harris 2024 + 0.15 × Trump 2024 For every 1,000 Trump points, 850 stayed Republican. 150 landed on the Democratic side. The numbers: Senate: Republicans kept ~85% of Trump's share Governor: ~86% Variation explained: 88% What makes it strange: It didn't matter how red the state was. Wyoming, Ohio, Illinois, Massachusetts: same 15% (p = 0.69). The Democratic gain was sized by Trump's vote, not Harris's. The Harris part is zero. The 0.85 beat both standard election-swing models in every set of races. Drop any single state and the multiplier stays between 0.846 and 0.853. Why seats flipped: At 0.85, any state where Trump got under about 58.5% falls below 50%. Texas, Kansas, Iowa, Ohio, South Carolina, North Carolina: all under that line. All flipped. What this means: Midterms are messy. This one isn't. Two sets of races, different candidates, different campaigns, and the same 15% decrease almost everywhere. A pattern this even demands answers at the ballot level: paper ballot hand counts, poll-book check-ins against votes counted, and voter files showing who actually turned out. The numbers raise the question. The ballots can answer it. This is what the image in the post looked like: (Image source: @TruthAnswersAll post on X.) The graphic was labeled: THE 15% BAND Republicans kept about 85% of Trump's 2024 share in race after race. States ordered reddest to bluest in 2024. It included both the Senate and the Governors' races for 2026 and drew lines claiming to show comparisons between Trump's 2024 result and the 2026 results, with additional lines drawn in showing the results of a formula that calculated a 15% decrease or increase from Donald Trump's or Kamala Harris' result in each state in 2024. The y-axis of the graph went from 20% to 80% and the graph was very elongated along the x-axis, making all lines appear relatively close together. Some people interpreted this graph and the post to mean there was a clean, uniform 85% percent ratio between Trump's 2024 result and the various Republican Senate candidates in 2026, for example here at The Gateway Pundit (archived here ): The theory, dubbed the \"85/15 Algorithm\" that was originally posted by Truth Is Freedom X account, suggests that Republican candidates received approximately 85% of President Trump's 2024 vote share. Lead Stories used 2024 Presidential Election data from the Wall Street Journal (archived here ) and the latest reported 2026 Senate data from the WSJ (archived here ) at the time of writing to attempt to recreate the graph. We also added three states that were missing from the original graph (Alaska, Arkansas and Montana). Note that in some states the counts are still ongoing and that our snapshot is from a different moment in time compared to the one in the X post, but the overall shape of the 2024 and 2026 lines is quite consistent between our graph and the one from \"Truth Is Freedom\". Omitting the data about the results for the Democrats and plotting the lines on a somewhat narrower, 0% to 80% graph, the actual variation in the 2026 results for the Republican senate candidates compared to the number from the formula based on 85% of Trump's 2024 result becomes much clearer: (Image source: Lead Stories) There is a stretch of red states on the left of the graph (West Virginia, Oklahoma, Alabama, Kentucky, Arkansas, Tennessee, Mississippi) where the 2026 result lines up to within a percent of the formula. But on either end of that stretch there are already differences: in Wyoming the Republican candidate did better and in Mississippi worse than what the formula said. The further to the right of the graph, the more variation there is and the bigger it becomes. In Maine the result for the 2026 Republican candidate was actually better than Trump's 2024 result. You can download our data here: Trump24-Senate2026-comparison.xlsx The graph did say the Republican candidates kept a certain percentage of Trump's share \"on average\" but there would be such an average number whatever the result in any state was and an average ultimately says nothing about how the results in various individual states are connected or not connected to other numbers in other individual elections or individual other states. To help understand this better, imagine the graphs don't represent elections but road trips by two different cars. One drove through thirty-odd states in 2024, the other made the trip in 2026. Knowing that on average the second car completed the full trip 15% slower than the first car tells you nothing about exactly how fast or slow the second car went in each state. Maybe there was less traffic this year in one state, or there were roadworks in another, or the car broke down and had to be towed at some point in one particular state. An average won't tell you that information. All you know is that the total trip took 15% longer. 2026-11-08T17:24:59Z 2026-11-08T17:24:59Z Added road trip analogy. Maarten Schenk is the co-founder and COO/CTO of Lead Stories and an expert on fake news and hoax websites. He likes to go beyond just debunking trending fake news stories and is endlessly fascinated by the dazzling variety of psychological and technical tricks used by the people and networks who intentionally spread made-up things on the internet. Read more about or contact Maarten Schenk Lead Stories is a fact checking website that is always looking for the latest false, misleading, deceptive or inaccurate stories, videos or images going viral on the internet. Spotted something? Let us know! . Lead Stories is a: Verified signatory of the IFCN Code of Principles Verified EFCSN member Founding sponsor of Indicator's Show & Tell Podcast Follow us on social media Subscribe to our newsletter * indicates required Email Address * Marketing Permissions Please select all the ways you would like to hear from Lead Stories LLC: Email You can unsubscribe at any time by clicking the link in the footer of our emails. For information about our privacy practices, please visit our website. We use Mailchimp as our marketing platform. By clicking below to subscribe, you acknowledge that your information will be transferred to Mailchimp for processing. Learn more about Mailchimp's privacy practices here. Get more fact-checks in your Google Search results by setting up leadstories.com as one of your preferred sources . AI Video Fact Check: FAKE Video From 'SUETA' Shows Damage To Kerch Strait Bridge After Ukrainian Drone Attack -- Video Is AI-Generated Fact Check AI Generated Fact Check: FAKE Video Shows Deploying U.S. Soldiers Kneeling In Prayer On A Runway Fact Check Fiction Story Fact Check: There Is NO Record Of A 1997 Interaction Between Bill Clinton And 'Marcus Williams', A Homeless Desert Storm Veteran -- Clickbait Fiction Fact Check Actors Acting Fact Check: Skit Of Passengers Screaming On A Plane While 'Unknown Creature Is Loose' Is NOT Real Fact Check More Variation Fact Check: NO Uniform '15% Band' Formula Mapping Trump's 2024 Result Exactly Onto 2026 Senate Race -- Results Show Variations Fact Check Misidentified Fact Check: She Did Have 4 Legs, But Josephine Myrtle Corbin Is NOT The Woman Pictured Fact Check IDs Unknown Fact Check: Video Claiming Florida School Bus Beating Of 'Lily Trejo' By 'Jake Lynch' Is Partially AI-Generated -- Names Unverified, Photos From Unrelated Cases Fact Check Music Video Fact Check: FAKE Palantir Ad Shows Margaret Qualley, Madelyn Cline, Sabrina Carpenter Fact Check Prewar Footage Fact Check: NO Evidence Video Confirms U.S. Is Withdrawing Troops from Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, Iraq, Jordan, UAE, Syria, Other Gulf Countries Fact Check AI Video Fact Check: FAKE UNN Video of Kerch Strait Bridge Is NOT Real Footage of Its Collapse Fact Check No Connection Fact Check: FBI Did NOT Say It Found Correspondence Between Abdul El-Sayed And Chinese Nationals Charged With Smuggling Pathogens Fact Check GTA VI 2026 Fact Check: FAKE Rockstar Games Letter Claims GTA VI Release Date Pushed Back To 2027 Fact Check AI Images Fact Check: Real AND AI-Generated Details In Trump Post About Markwayne Mullin's Son Jim -- Separating True From Fake Fact Check AI Video Fact Check: FAKE AI-Generated 'Sueta' Video Shows Damage To Kerch Strait Bridge After Ukrainian Attack Fact Check Share Tweet Go",
